In JavaScript, declare variables using `let` (mutable value) or `const` (cannot be reassigned):

~~~js
const author = "lan.code";   // chuỗi (string)
let likes = 0;               // số (number)
const isPublic = true;       // đúng/sai (boolean)
likes = likes + 1;           // likes bây giờ là 1
~~~

Default usage rule: **always use `const`**, only switch to `let` when reassignment is truly necessary.

`if / else if / else` allows the program to choose a path:

~~~js
const text = "Xin chào Nhịp";
if (text.length === 0) {
  console.log("Hãy viết gì đó");
} else if (text.length > 500) {
  console.log("Tối đa 500 ký tự");
} else {
  console.log("Đăng được");
}
~~~

`===` compares for **strict equality** (same value and same type). Always use `===`, not `==`.

If there are 3 posts, print 3 times. Don't manually write 3 lines — use a loop:

~~~js
const posts = ["Chào cả nhà", "Hôm nay học HTML", "Ai học Coding không?"];
for (const post of posts) {
  console.log(post);
}
~~~

`for...of` iterates through **each element** of the list. Whether 3 posts or 3.000 posts, the code remains just three lines.

A function encapsulates a task under a name, which can be called any number of times:

~~~js
function checkPost(text) {
  if (text.length === 0) return "Hãy viết gì đó";
  if (text.length > 500) return "Tối đa 500 ký tự";
  return "ok";
}

console.log(checkPost(""));          // Hãy viết gì đó
console.log(checkPost("Xin chào"));  // ok
~~~

`text` is a **parameter** — a value passed in. `return` returns the result to the function call site.

~~~css
.post {
  display: flex;      /* con xếp thành hàng */
  gap: 12px;          /* khoảng cách giữa các con */
  align-items: flex-start;
}
.post-body { flex: 1; min-width: 0; }  /* chiếm phần còn lại */
~~~

`min-width: 0` allows the body to shrink — without it, a very long word would push the entire page off-screen.

~~~css
:root {
  --bg: #ffffff;
  --text: #101010;
  --muted: #5f5f5f;     /* vẫn đạt 4,5:1 trên nền trắng */
  --accent: #0a66ff;
  --line: #e5e5e5;
  --space: 12px;
}
.post { padding: var(--space); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
~~~

Changing one variable changes the entire page. Next step: redefine variables in `@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)` to instantly get dark mode.

~~~css
/* mặc định: điện thoại */
.feed { padding: 0 12px; }

/* từ 700px trở lên: máy tính bảng, máy tính */
@media (min-width: 700px) {
  .feed { padding: 0; }
  .composer { border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 16px; }
}
~~~

Test with DevTools: click the phone icon (Toggle device toolbar) to view the page at various screen sizes.

~~~js
const add = (a, b) => a + b;          // trả về ngay
const greet = (name) => {
  const message = `Chào ${name}`;     // chỉ sống trong hàm
  return message;
};
console.log(message);                 // ReferenceError
~~~

Variables declared with `let`/`const` only live within the nearest pair of curly braces (**block scope**). The shorter a variable's life, the fewer places it can be accidentally modified.

~~~js
const mine   = posts.filter((p) => p.author === "lan.code");  // lọc
const texts  = posts.map((p) => p.text);                      // biến đổi
const first  = posts.find((p) => p.id === 2);                 // tìm một
const total  = posts.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p.likes, 0);    // gộp
const newest = [...posts].sort(
  (a, b) => new Date(b.createdAt) - new Date(a.createdAt)
);
~~~

Note: `sort` **modifies the original array**, so copy it first with `[...posts]`.

~~~js
const created = new Date("2026-10-27T09:30:00");
const minutes = Math.floor((Date.now() - created) / 60000);
~~~

Subtract two `Date` to get **milliseconds**. Dividing 60.000 gives minutes. To show "5 minutes ago" in the correct language, use the browser's built-in standard library — `Intl.RelativeTimeFormat` — instead of building the text yourself:

~~~js
const rtf = new Intl.RelativeTimeFormat("vi", { numeric: "auto" });
rtf.format(-5, "minute");   // "5 phút trước"
~~~

~~~js
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();      // chặn tải lại trang
  const text = form.content.value;
  // ...
});

textarea.addEventListener("input", () => {
  counter.textContent = `${textarea.value.length}/500`;
});
~~~

`submit` catches both button clicks and pressing Enter in a form. Always catch the form's `submit` instead of the button's `click`.

~~~js
postsBox.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
  const button = event.target.closest(".like");
  if (!button) return;                     // bấm chỗ khác
  const id = Number(button.closest("article").dataset.id);
  // thả tim bài có id này
});
~~~

`closest` walks up the tree to find the parent element that matches the selector. `data-id` on `article` reads through `dataset.id`. Remember it's a **string**, and convert it to a number with `Number()`.

~~~php
<?php
declare(strict_types=1);          // dòng đầu tiên sau <?php

function check_post(string $body): ?string
{
    $body = trim($body);
    if ($body === '') {
        return 'Hãy viết gì đó.';
    }
    return null;                  // ?string: trả chuỗi HOẶC null
}

check_post(123);  // TypeError: phải truyền string
~~~

- Parameter and return types help PHP **report errors early** instead of silently coercing types.
- `strict_types` takes effect **only in the file that declares it**, so every `.php` file of Nhịp begins with this line.
- Arrow functions: `fn (array $p): bool => $p['author'] === $username`, same as `(p) => ...` of JavaScript.

Text the user types goes straight into HTML — that's an XSS vulnerability, the same as `innerHTML` in step 6, except this time it's on the server. The standard function for PHP:

~~~php
htmlspecialchars('<b>hi</b>', ENT_QUOTES | ENT_SUBSTITUTE, 'UTF-8');
// &lt;b&gt;hi&lt;/b&gt;
~~~

- `ENT_QUOTES`: replaces both `'` and `"` — required when inserting into an `value="..."` attribute.
- `ENT_SUBSTITUTE`: a malformed UTF-8 string does not make the function return empty.

Typing this whole line every time means you'll forget sometimes, so Nhịp wraps it in a short function **`e()`**. The rule from here to the end of the course: **every value output into HTML goes through `e()`**, except numbers already cast `(int)`.

Deleting an account may take several statements. If the second one fails, the data is left half-done. A **transaction** groups them together:

~~~sql
START TRANSACTION;
DELETE FROM likes   WHERE user_id = 5;
DELETE FROM follows WHERE follower_id = 5 OR followee_id = 5;
DELETE FROM users   WHERE id = 5;
COMMIT;        -- ghi tất cả; gặp lỗi thì ROLLBACK để huỷ tất cả
~~~

Four **ACID** properties: *Atomicity* (all or nothing), *Consistency* (valid state), *Isolation* (separate), *Durability* (persistent). InnoDB guarantees all four. Step 11 calls a transaction from PHP using `beginTransaction()` / `commit()` / `rollBack()`.

~~~php
// Chỗ trống vị trí: ?  — truyền mảng theo thứ tự
$stmt = db()->prepare('SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ?');
$stmt->execute([$username]);

// Chỗ trống đặt tên: :ten — truyền mảng kết hợp, dễ đọc khi nhiều tham số
$stmt = db()->prepare('INSERT INTO posts (user_id, body) VALUES (:user_id, :body)');
$stmt->execute(['user_id' => 1, 'body' => $body]);
~~~

- Don't put quotes around the blank: write `= ?`, **not** `= '?'`.
- Don't mix `?` and `:ten` in the same sentence.
- Each blank replaces **one value**. `WHERE id IN (?)` with an array of 3 elements must produce enough `IN (?, ?, ?)` to fill it.

`LIMIT` needs an integer: use `bindValue(':limit', $n, PDO::PARAM_INT)` before `execute()`, just as `feed_posts()`.

~~~php
$pdo = db();
$pdo->beginTransaction();
try {
    $pdo->prepare('DELETE FROM likes WHERE post_id = ?')->execute([$id]);
    $pdo->prepare('DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = ? AND user_id = ?')->execute([$id, $me]);
    $pdo->commit();
} catch (Throwable $e) {
    $pdo->rollBack();   // một lệnh lỗi → huỷ tất cả
    throw $e;           // ném tiếp để không nuốt lỗi
}
~~~

Thanks to `ERRMODE_EXCEPTION`, any SQL error lands in `catch`. In Nhịp, the foreign key `ON DELETE CASCADE` already takes care of deleting the likes along with the post, so a transaction is only really needed at step 22 (creating the post, its hashtag tags and the notification all at once).

~~~php
// Đăng ký
$hash = password_hash($password, PASSWORD_DEFAULT);
// lưu $hash vào users.password_hash

// Đăng nhập
if (password_verify($password, $hashTừCơSởDữLiệu)) {
    // đúng mật khẩu
}

// Thuật toán/chi phí mặc định thay đổi theo phiên bản PHP?
if (password_needs_rehash($hash, PASSWORD_DEFAULT)) {
    // băm lại bằng cấu hình mới, lưu đè
}
~~~

`PASSWORD_DEFAULT` currently uses bcrypt. PHP 8.4 raises the default cost from 10 to **12** — that's why the hash in `seed.sql` (generated with PHP 8.4) starts with `$2y$12$`, while accounts registered on XAMPP 8.2 are `$2y$10$`. `password_needs_rehash` automatically syncs them each time a user signs in.

~~~php
session_set_cookie_params([
    'lifetime' => 0,         // cookie phiên: mất khi đóng trình duyệt
    'path'     => '/',
    'httponly' => true,      // document.cookie không thấy nó
    'samesite' => 'Lax',     // không đi kèm POST khởi phát từ trang khác
    // 'secure' => true,     // chỉ gửi qua HTTPS — bật khi lên hosting (step 24)
]);
session_name('nhip_session');
session_start();
~~~

- `session_start()` must run **before** any character is printed to the page (including a space before `<?php`), because it sends the `Set-Cookie` header. Classic error: *"headers already sent"*.
- Set a custom session name (`nhip_session`) instead of the default `PHPSESSID` so it doesn't clash with other projects on the same `localhost`.

~~~php
function login_user(int $userId): void
{
    session_regenerate_id(true);    // cấp mã mới, xoá tệp phiên cũ
    $_SESSION['user_id'] = $userId;
}
~~~

Attack scenario if the first line is missing: an attacker opens Nhịp, gets a `abc` code, and tries to plant `abc` in the victim's browser. The victim signs in → the `abc` session now belongs to the victim's account → the attacker now holds `abc`.

For `session_regenerate_id(true)`, at the moment of sign-in the `abc` code is invalidated, so the victim receives a new code the attacker doesn't know.

~~~php
$id = filter_var($_GET['id'] ?? null, FILTER_VALIDATE_INT, [
    'options' => ['min_range' => 1],
]);
// "3" → 3 · "3abc" → false · "-1" → false · "" → false · mảng → false
~~~

Compared with `(int) $_GET['id']`: casting variable `"3abc"` to 3 and `"abc"` to 0 silently “fixes” bad data into good data. `FILTER_VALIDATE_INT` rejects it outright, and `id_param()` turns that rejection into a **404** page.

Principle: **validate and reject**, don't **guess the intent and fix** the input data.

~~~php
$stmt = db()->prepare('DELETE FROM posts WHERE id = ? AND user_id = ?');
$stmt->execute([$id, $me['id']]);
~~~

The foreign key at step 9 has `ON DELETE CASCADE`, so deleting a post will **automatically delete** all its hearts and all its replies (along with their hearts and replies, cascading all the way down the tree). No extra statements are needed.

**Soft delete** is another option: add a `deleted_at` column and only hide the post. Pros: recoverable, keeps evidence when there are complaints. Cons: every query must remember to add `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL`. Nhịp chose hard delete for simplicity; the project completed at step 26 could add soft delete as a new feature.

~~~php
$mime = (new finfo(FILEINFO_MIME_TYPE))->file($file['tmp_name']);
// đọc vài byte đầu: FF D8 FF → image/jpeg, 89 50 4E 47 → image/png

if (!isset(AVATAR_TYPES[$mime]) || getimagesize($file['tmp_name']) === false) {
    throw new RuntimeException('Chỉ nhận ảnh JPG, PNG hoặc WebP.');
}
~~~

- `finfo` detects the type from the **file signature**, not the extension.
- `getimagesize` tries to read the image dimensions — a file that isn't a real image fails.
- `AVATAR_TYPES` is an **allowlist** mapping type → extension. The saved file's extension comes from this list, never from the name the user sent.

The size limit has two layers: `upload_max_filesize` in `php.ini` (XAMPP default 40 MB) and `AVATAR_MAX_BYTES` of Nhịp (2 MB).

~~~js
const response = await fetch(form.action, {
  method: "POST",
  body: new FormData(form),                     // gói mọi <input> của biểu mẫu
  headers: { Accept: "application/json" },      // "tôi muốn JSON"
});
const data = await response.json();             // chuỗi JSON → đối tượng
if (!response.ok) { /* 4xx, 5xx */ }
~~~

- `fetch` **only throws an error on network failure**. The server returning 401 or 500 still counts as a "success" at the network level, so you must check `response.ok` yourself (correct for codes 200–299).
- `FormData` sends the session cookie automatically (same origin), so PHP still knows who is clicking.
- Disable `button.disabled = true` while waiting: this blocks double clicks from sending two requests.
- The `catch` block calls `form.submit()`. On network error, fall back to the non-JavaScript approach.

~~~php
$wantsJson = str_contains($_SERVER['HTTP_ACCEPT'] ?? '', 'application/json');

$result = toggle_like($me['id'], $post['id']);

if ($wantsJson) {
    header('Content-Type: application/json');
    echo json_encode($result);              // {"liked":true,"likes":5}
    exit;
}
redirect('post.php?id=' . $post['id']);     // trình duyệt thường
~~~

Errors also follow two paths: if not logged in → JSON `401` (JavaScript automatically redirects to `login.php`), or redirects like every other page. Set `Content-Type: application/json` so the browser and `response.json()` interpret it correctly; `json_encode` by default escapes accented characters into `\u00e3` — valid, and JavaScript reads back the correct characters.

~~~php
$insert = db()->prepare('INSERT IGNORE INTO likes (user_id, post_id) VALUES (?, ?)');
$insert->execute([$userId, $postId]);
$liked = $insert->rowCount() === 1;        // thêm được → vừa thả tim
if (!$liked) {                              // đã có sẵn → lần bấm này là bỏ tim
    db()->prepare('DELETE FROM likes WHERE user_id = ? AND post_id = ?')
        ->execute([$userId, $postId]);
}
~~~

Don't `SELECT` first to ask "has it been liked yet": two parallel requests can both read "not yet" and then both insert. `INSERT IGNORE` plus a composite primary key lets the **database** decide atomically.

The returned like count is **recounted** from the table, not incremented or decremented 1 on the client, since others may like at the same time.

The feed has 20 posts; we need to know which posts the viewer has liked. The **wrong** way: run one query per post — 20 queries (the N+1 problem, 21 steps).

The right way — one query:

~~~php
$ids = [11, 10, 7, 5];
$marks = implode(',', array_fill(0, count($ids), '?'));     // "?,?,?,?"
$stmt = db()->prepare("SELECT post_id FROM likes WHERE user_id = ? AND post_id IN ($marks)");
$stmt->execute([$viewerId, ...$ids]);
~~~

The `$marks` string concatenated into SQL contains only `?` and commas **generated by the code** — no user data goes into it, so it stays safe. The `...` operator spreads the array of ids into parameters. `array_flip` turns the list of liked ids into an instant `isset()` lookup table.

~~~php
$build = function (int $parentId) use (&$build, $byParent): array {
    $nodes = $byParent[$parentId] ?? [];
    foreach ($nodes as &$node) {
        $node['children'] = $build((int) $node['id']);    // gọi lại chính nó
    }
    return $nodes;
};
~~~

Every recursive function needs two things: **a base case** (no children → return an empty array) and **a step that moves closer to the base case** (each call goes one level down).

- `use (&$build)`: an anonymous function that needs to call itself must receive the variable that holds it **by reference**.
- `foreach ($nodes as &$node)`: modify an element of the array directly.

`render_replies()` is recursive in exactly the same way: print one `<li>`, then call itself again for the `children` of that lesson.

~~~php
function csrf_token(): string
{
    if (empty($_SESSION['csrf'])) {
        $_SESSION['csrf'] = bin2hex(random_bytes(32));   // 64 ký tự hex, không đoán được
    }
    return $_SESSION['csrf'];
}

function csrf_field(): string
{
    return '<input type="hidden" name="_token" value="' . e(csrf_token()) . '">';
}
~~~

Generate the token **once** per session and reuse it for every form. This is secure enough for most applications. (Stronger option: a token per form, but it is more complex and breaks easily when multiple tabs are open.)

Each `<form method="post">` adds one line right after the opening tag:

~~~php
<form method="post" action="delete.php">
  <?= csrf_field() ?>
  ...
~~~

~~~php
function csrf_check(): void
{
    if ($_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'] !== 'POST') {
        return;                                   // GET không đổi dữ liệu, không cần
    }
    $sent = (string) ($_POST['_token'] ?? '');
    if ($sent === '' || !hash_equals(csrf_token(), $sent)) {
        abort(419, 'Phiên làm việc đã hết hạn hoặc yêu cầu không hợp lệ. Hãy tải lại trang.');
    }
}
~~~

**Why `hash_equals` instead of `===`?** A normal string comparison stops at the first differing character. The time it takes reveals how many leading characters matched, which in theory lets an attacker guess the token one character at a time. `hash_equals` always compares every character, in constant time.

Only **POST** requests are checked. GET must always be safe (step 13), so it carries no token, and GET-based search still lets links be shared.

~~~php
function client_ip(): ?string
{
    $ip = $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'] ?? '';
    $packed = @inet_pton($ip);          // "127.0.0.1" → 4 byte; IPv6 → 16 byte
    return $packed === false ? null : $packed;
}
~~~

`$_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR']` is the IP the server **actually** sees. **Do not** trust `X-Forwarded-For` or `X-Real-IP` from headers. An attacker can set them freely and spoof an IP to evade the limit. Trust that header only when a trusted proxy (CDN) sets it, and it must be configured separately (outside the scope of this lock).

Stored in binary form: `INSERT … (ip) VALUES (?)` with the value `inet_pton`. Read back in SQL: `INET6_NTOA(ip)`.

~~~php
function login_attempt_count(): int
{
    $stmt = db()->prepare('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM login_attempts
        WHERE ip = ? AND created_at > (NOW() - INTERVAL ? MINUTE)');
    $stmt->execute([client_ip(), LOGIN_WINDOW_MIN]);
    return (int) $stmt->fetchColumn();
}
~~~

`NOW() - INTERVAL 15 MINUTE` is the point 15 minutes ago; only attempts after that point are counted. The window is **sliding** in real time, not "reset at the top of the hour".

In `login.php`, check **before** calling `verify_login` (this saves one hash operation):

~~~php
if (login_attempt_count() >= LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS) {
    $error = 'Bạn đã thử sai quá nhiều lần. Hãy đợi 15 phút rồi thử lại.';
} else {
    // verify_login, ghi nhận nếu sai, xoá nếu đúng
}
~~~

~~~php
function install_error_handler(bool $debug): void
{
    if ($debug) return;                 // dev: giữ lỗi chi tiết để sửa
    set_exception_handler(function (Throwable $e): void {
        error_log('[nhip] ' . $e);      // chi tiết vào LOG máy chủ
        http_response_code(500);
        echo '<!DOCTYPE html>…Có lỗi xảy ra. Hãy thử lại sau.…';  // ra trình duyệt: chung chung
        exit;
    });
}
~~~

Two different audiences for the same error: **users** see a safe message, **administrators** read the details in the log. This is exactly how to handle **A10 Mishandling of Exceptional Conditions**: an error must not leak information, nor leave the system in an undefined state.

`debug` in `config.php`: `true` on your machine, `false` on hosting (step 24).

~~~php
function dispatch(): void
{
    $method = $_SERVER['REQUEST_METHOD'];
    $path = parse_url($_SERVER['REQUEST_URI'], PHP_URL_PATH);
    $path = '/' . ltrim(substr($path, strlen(base_path())), '/');  // bỏ phần /nhip/public

    foreach (ROUTES as [$m, $pattern, $page, $name]) {
        $regex = '#^' . preg_replace('/\{(\w+)\}/', '(?<$1>[^/]+)', $pattern) . '$#';
        if ($m === $method && preg_match($regex, $path, $params)) {
            $GLOBALS['route_params'] = $params;
            require __DIR__ . '/pages/' . $page . '.php';
            return;
        }
    }
    abort(404, 'Trang không tồn tại.');
}
~~~

`{id}` is changed to a capture group `(?<id>[^/]+)` — `preg_match` fills in `$params['id']`. If no route matches → **404** (fail-safe: deny by default).

Each controller in `src/pages/` now **no longer** has `require bootstrap` (the front controller handles it), reads parameters from the router, calls the Model, then loads the View:

~~~php
// src/pages/post.php (rút gọn)
$id = id_param(['id' => route_param('id')]);
$post = find_post($id);
if ($post === null) abort(404, 'Bài này không còn tồn tại.');
// … xử lý POST (trả lời) …
$title = 'Bài của @' . $post['username'];
require view('header');
// … HTML …
require view('footer');
~~~

`view('header')` returns the path `src/views/header.php`. Controller and View live in two directories, but share the same variable scope when `require` — so the View sees `$title`, `$post` without passing them manually.

`LIMIT 20 OFFSET 2000` (page 101) forces the database to read and then **discard** 2000 rows. The deeper the page, the slower it gets (step 10). Social networks use **keyset cursors**: remember the last post on the previous page, then fetch posts **older than it**.

~~~php
function feed_posts(int $limit = 20, ?array $before = null): array
{
    $cursor = $before ? 'AND (p.created_at, p.id) < (:before_at, :before_id)' : '';
    $sql = FEED_SQL . " WHERE p.parent_id IS NULL $cursor
                        GROUP BY p.id ORDER BY p.created_at DESC, p.id DESC
                        LIMIT :limit";
    // lấy $limit + 1 để biết CÒN trang sau không
}
~~~

Comparing the **pair** `(created_at, id) < (…, …)` correctly handles cases where several posts share the same timestamp. The `idx_posts_feed` index lets this step jump straight to the right spot, as fast as the first page, even on page 1000.

The controller fetches `limit + 1` posts: if it receives the full 21, that means **there is** a next page, so it shows a "Load more" button with the cursor set to post number 20:

~~~php
$posts = feed_posts(20, $before);
$hasMore = count($posts) > 20;
$posts = array_slice($posts, 0, 20);
// link: url('feed', ['before' => $last['created_at'] . '|' . $last['id']])
~~~

The cursor in the address is **validated with a regex** before being trusted (data from the client, step 16). Because the URL carries the cursor, the 2 page can still be shared and bookmarked.

~~~php
function extract_tags(string $body): array
{
    preg_match_all('/#([\p{L}\p{N}_]{1,50})/u', $body, $m);
    return array_values(array_unique(array_map('mb_strtolower', $m[1])));
}

function extract_mentions(string $body): array
{
    preg_match_all('/@([a-z0-9._]{3,30})/', $body, $m);
    return array_values(array_unique($m[1]));
}
~~~

- `preg_match_all` returns **all** matches; `$m[1]` is the array of capture-group parts (excluding `#`/`@`).
- `array_unique` removes duplicates (typing `#php #php` counts only once).
- Tags are lowercased so that `#PHP` and `#php` are treated as the same.

~~~php
function attach_tags(int $postId, array $tags): void
{
    foreach ($tags as $name) {
        db()->prepare('INSERT IGNORE INTO tags (name) VALUES (?)')->execute([$name]);
        $id = db()->prepare('SELECT id FROM tags WHERE name = ?');
        $id->execute([$name]);
        db()->prepare('INSERT IGNORE INTO post_tags (post_id, tag_id) VALUES (?, ?)')
            ->execute([$postId, (int) $id->fetchColumn()]);
    }
}
~~~

`INSERT IGNORE` for `tags`: if the tag already exists, skip it; no duplicate-key error on `UNIQUE`. `INSERT IGNORE` for `post_tags`: if the pair already exists, skip it (composite primary key). Same idempotent technique as "Follow" (step 14).

Page `/tag/php`: `FEED_SQL` + `JOIN post_tags JOIN tags WHERE t.name = 'php'`.

Publishing a post now involves several write steps: create the post, attach tags, send mention notifications, and notify the original post's owner. If step three fails, we **don't** want to be left with a post that has no tags. Wrap everything in **one transaction** (step 11):

~~~php
function publish_post(int $userId, string $body, ?int $parentId = null): int
{
    $pdo = db();
    $pdo->beginTransaction();
    try {
        $postId = $parentId === null
            ? create_post($userId, $body)
            : create_reply($userId, $parentId, $body);
        attach_tags($postId, extract_tags($body));
        notify_mentions($body, $userId, $postId);
        if ($parentId !== null) {
            $parent = find_post($parentId);
            if ($parent) notify((int) $parent['user_id'], $userId, 'reply', $parentId);
        }
        $pdo->commit();
        return $postId;
    } catch (Throwable $e) {
        $pdo->rollBack();   // một bước hỏng → huỷ tất cả
        throw $e;
    }
}
~~~

`feed.php` and `post.php` now call `publish_post` instead of the bare `create_post`/`create_reply`.

~~~php
function render_body(string $body): string
{
    $safe = e($body);                                   // 1. THOÁT trước
    $safe = preg_replace_callback('/#([\p{L}\p{N}_]{1,50})/u', function ($m) {
        return '<a class="tag" href="' . e(url('tag', ['tag' => mb_strtolower($m[1])]))
             . '">#' . $m[1] . '</a>';                   // 2. nối link SAU
    }, $safe);
    $safe = preg_replace_callback('/@([a-z0-9._]{3,30})/', function ($m) {
        return '<a class="mention" href="' . e(url('profile', ['username' => $m[1]])) . '">@' . $m[1] . '</a>';
    }, $safe);
    return nl2br($safe, false);
}
~~~

Order is everything: escape HTML **first**, so that `<script>` in the post becomes harmless `&lt;script&gt;`. Only then add the `<a>` we generate. The post card calls `render_body($post['body'])` instead of `e($post['body'])`. `nl2br` preserves line breaks.

~~~sql
SELECT DATE(created_at) AS d, COUNT(*) AS n
FROM posts WHERE created_at > (NOW() - INTERVAL 7 DAY)
GROUP BY DATE(created_at);
~~~

This query **only returns days that have posts**. If no one posts on day 22/10, that day disappears, and the chart will "jump" from 21 to 23, which is misleading.

A simple fix (done in PHP): collect the results into an array by day, then **loop over all 7 days** and fill any missing day with 0:

~~~php
for ($i = 6; $i >= 0; $i--) {
    $d = date('Y-m-d', strtotime("-$i day"));
    $series[] = ['day' => $d, 'n' => $byDay[$d] ?? 0];
}
~~~

The statistics page shows the product's overall picture, so **not** everyone may view it:

~~~php
$me = require_login();
if ((int) $me['id'] !== STATS_OWNER_ID) {
    abort(403, 'Chỉ chủ sản phẩm xem được trang này.');
}
~~~

Nhịp has no dedicated "admin" role, so for now the **first account** (id 1) is treated as the product owner. A real system would have a `role` column in `users` and check `role = 'admin'`, but the principle of **checking permissions on the server for each page** is the same (step 13, 16).
